w on the Silk Road trade routes.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/bamako"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/bamako.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Bamako</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Bamako is the capital and largest city of Mali, on the Niger River in the south-west of the country.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/bamangwato"><span class="enc-name">BaMangwato</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The BaMangwato are a Tswana people of present-day Botswana.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/bamar-people"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/bamar-people.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Bamar people</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Bamar, or Burmans, are the largest ethnic group of Myanmar, forming about two-thirds of its population and living mainly in the central Irrawaddy valley.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/bambandyanalo"><span class="enc-name">Bambandyanalo</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Bambandyanalo, known to archaeologists as K2, is an archaeological site in the Limpopo valley of northern South Africa, near Mapungubwe Hill.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/bambara-people"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/bambara-people.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Bambara people</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Bambara, or Bamana, are a Mande people of the upper Niger valley in Mali and the country's largest ethnic group.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/bambatha-rebellion"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclo